<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/advanced/cardiovascular-pathophysiology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cardiovascular disease is the world's leading cause of death (~18 million deaths/year) — and
    largely a <strong>decades-long, preventable process</strong>. The pathophysiology chain:
    risk factors damage vessels → plaques grow silently → rupture or pump failure strikes. The
    medicine is mostly arithmetic applied early (pressure, lipids, glucose, smoking) rather than
    heroics late.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain from risk factor to event — and where interventions bite" widget="equationStep" config={cardioCfg} />

  <h2>Connecting the physiology</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The heart-and-circulation lesson</strong> explains the pump and the plumbing; pathophysiology explains what goes wrong: valves narrow or leak, muscle weakens, rhythm circuits misfire (fibrillation — treated with the defibrillator's reset).</li>
    <li><strong>ECG reading is pattern recognition:</strong> the electrical trace reveals rhythm, muscle damage (ST elevation in heart attacks) and conduction blocks — a 12-lead ECG is the fastest life-saving test in medicine.</li>
    <li><strong>Risk calculators quantify decades:</strong> models combine age, pressure, cholesterol, smoking and diabetes into 10-year risk percentages — turning prevention from vibes into numbers (Framingham study, running since 1948, built the framework).</li>
    <li><strong>Prevention beats rescue:</strong> population blood-pressure control saves more lives than any emergency department. The public-health lesson and this one share the conclusion from different ends.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Braunwald's Heart Disease; Framingham Heart Study (1948–); ESC/AHA guidelines; GBD cardiovascular estimates (Lancet).</p>
</TopicPage>
